# Part II — Representation-Level Frontiers

## §11. Non-textual source forms: text as one projection

The empirical evidence that **text is a lossy projection of programs,
not the canonical form**, is now substantial:

- **JetBrains MPS** (Meta Programming System) is a production
  projectional editor used for industrial DSLs (Siemens medical
  devices, automotive); the underlying model is a typed AST with
  multiple projections (tabular, mathematical notation, diagrammatic).
  The fact that an industrial company can build a several-million-
  line projectional codebase establishes feasibility.
- **Hazel** (Omar, Hammer, Aldrich, et al., POPL 2017 "Hazelnut: A
  Bidirectionally Typed Structure Editor Calculus") is the academic
  state of the art. Hazel's typed holes mean *every editor state is
  a well-typed program*; there is no "syntactically invalid"
  intermediate. The calculus is formalized. Live programming is a
  property of the language, not an IDE feature.
- **Lamdu** (Eyal Lotem) — typed structure editor with live
  evaluation; less academic, more usable demos.
- **Unison** (Chiusano, Bjarnason; see also §12) — text is the *input
  format* but the canonical form is the typed AST in the codebase
  database. The text is regenerated.
- **Bret Victor's body of work** — "Learnable Programming," "Drawing
  Dynamic Visualizations," "Up and Down the Ladder of Abstraction" —
  argues that the *medium of programming* is the bottleneck, not the
  syntax.
- **Dynamicland** (Victor's research group, Oakland; halted public-
  facing but research continues) — physical-object-based programming.
- **Field** (Marc Downie / OpenEndedGroup) — code/graphics/timeline
  integrated programming surface.
- **Glamorous Toolkit / Pharo** (Feenk) — moldable development where
  every object has multiple custom inspectors and the IDE is
  generated from the program.
- **Luna** / **Enso** — dual textual/visual representations of the
  same dataflow program.

The unifying theoretical move is **bidirectional / structural editing
as the canonical interface, with text as one projection.** The
Hazelnut calculus is the most rigorous reference. The conclusion for
a sovereign language: the parser and pretty-printer are *projections*
into and out of a canonical typed-hole-bearing AST, and there is no
privileged textual representation.

## §12. Content-addressed code (Unison and beyond)

Unison's design (Chiusano, Bjarnason; unison-lang.org docs, "the big
idea"; LWN article "Programming in Unison," 2024) is now well-
documented:

- Every typed AST node has a **512-bit SHA-3 hash** of its structure,
  with named-variable references replaced by positional De Bruijn
  indices and dependencies replaced by *their* hashes.
- The hash *is* the identity. Names are metadata in a sidecar
  namespace.
- Renaming is a metadata operation, not a code change. Merges never
  conflict on rename. There are no builds — code is compiled once
  and cached by hash, forever.
- Tests are cached by hash: if the function and all its transitive
  dependencies have not changed (i.e., same hash), the test result
  is reused.
- Distribution: code can be sent over the wire by hash, and a remote
  node fetches missing definitions on demand. This is the substrate
  that makes Unison's distributed-programming story work —
  `Remote.at(location, fn)` ships the function (just hashes plus
  missing definitions) to the location.

The structural insight: **the Unison codebase is a Merkle DAG.** This
is structurally identical to Nix (content-addressed builds; Dolstra's
2006 thesis is the reference) and IPFS (content-addressed storage)
and Git (content-addressed snapshots) and Pijul/Darcs (patch theory —
algebraic compositions of changes rather than snapshots).

The compounding effects for SIGIL:
- A **global codebase** indexed by hash, where any function ever
  defined is reachable as a constant. This is the "Hoogle for all of
  programming" idea, but it works because the hash *is* the address.
- **Provenance is free**: every value's compute graph (per §27) is a
  DAG of hashes, so you can prove what produced any output.
- **Caching is global**: if anyone in the world has ever computed
  `f(x)` where both `f` and `x` are content-addressed, the result is
  reachable. (Memoization across organizations becomes a real
  possibility, modulo capability-secure access to the cache.)
- **Patch theory** (Pijul, based on Mimram's category-theoretic
  framework, ICFP 2014) provides a sound algebra of *changes* on top
  of content-addressed snapshots. A sovereign language can adopt
  this as the version-control primitive built into the language,
  eliminating Git as a foreign dependency.

The interaction with §17 (AI-native source): when functions are
content-addressed, a neural compiler that proposes refactorings can
express its proposal as **"replace hash A with hash B, where A and B
have the same type"** — a structural diff that is unambiguous and
verifiable.

## §13. Multi-presentation source: one canonical AST, many surfaces

If the canonical form is a typed AST (per §11), the surface
presentations multiply:

- **Prose / literate**: Knuth's WEB, Org-babel, Jupyter, Quarto —
  already mainstream.
- **Dense glyphs**: APL (Iverson, 1962, Turing Award lecture
  "Notation as a Tool of Thought," 1979), J (Iverson, 1990s), K / Q
  (Whitney, KX Systems), BQN (Lochbaum, 2020), Uiua (Kaspar
  Mortensen, 2023). These are not just terse syntax — the
  *concision* changes what is thinkable. Uiua specifically is stack-
  based plus rank-polymorphic, and reads top-to-bottom right-to-
  left — a different cognitive model.
- **Graphs**: dataflow editors (TouchDesigner, Houdini's VOPs/SOPs,
  Max/MSP, PureData, Blender's geometry nodes, Unreal Blueprints).
  The Unison codebase's underlying form is already a graph;
  rendering it as a graph is just a projection choice.
- **Voice**: not yet mainstream; relevant for accessibility and for
  AI agents that produce structured speech outputs.
- **VR/AR spatial**: Krea, Hover, recent immersive coding work.
  Speculative as a primary surface, plausible for visualization.
- **Diagrammatic** (Bret Victor's "Drawing Dynamic Visualizations"
  talk, 2013) — code as direct manipulation of the data being
  computed.

For SIGIL: **the language's reference manual is itself the cleanest
projection.** Multiple surfaces over a single canonical form means
the manual can render as glyphs for experts, as named-keyword text
for newcomers, as a graph for system architecture, and as a Bret-
Victor live visualization for tutorial purposes — all from the same
source.

The non-obvious adjacency: **dense glyph notation (APL family) is
highly amenable to AI generation.** A model that knows the BQN/Uiua
primitive set can express a transformation in 4-8 tokens that would
be a 200-token Python function. If the language is a partner to AI
agents (per the Prime Directive), there is a real argument for first-
class support for a dense-glyph surface.
